Lost in the Inbox
A Problem that buries what matters than what doesn't.

My inbox mixes everything together — academic notices, promotions, newsletters,
random updates etc.So the mails that actually matter gets buried under the ones that don't.

I end up spending time skimming through low-priority mails just to find the one email from a
professor or the Program office.

Replying to simple routine mails (confirmations, RSVPs, acknowledgements) or scheduling calendar
invitations or drafting meeting plans take up time that I'd rather spend elsewhere, even though 
the reply itself is quick and predictable.

What it solves 
Filters my inbox to surface only academic-related updates — professors, coursework, deadlines,
university announcements front and center.

Everything else gets summarized into a quick glance as a notification instead of a full inbox 
scroll, so nothing needs to be read in full unless I want to.

For routine mails, it drafts a reply automatically and just asks "send this?". A yes sends it
immediately, no typing required and a no either decline the request or can be modified or 
personalized accordingly.

This is my final capstone idea 
